#   C(M,N) += A(M,K_local) @ B(K_local,N)      (accumulated via atomic add)
#
# Layout assumptions (all row-major, contiguous):
#   A : (M, K_full)   element (m,k)      -> A[m*K_full + k]
#   B : (K_full, N)   element (k,n)      -> B[k*N + n]
#   C : (M, N)        element (m,n)      -> C[m*N + n]
#
# Grid:  (M/32, N/32, K_split)
# Block: (32, 32, 1)   -> one thread per output element of a 32x32 CTA tile
#
# Each CTA loads 32x32 tiles of A and B into shared memory, iterates over
# K_local in steps of 32, and accumulates a partial dot-product.  The final
# partial result is atomically added into the (pre-zeroed) global C tensor,
# implementing the split-K reduction across blockIdx.z.
#
# Optimization applied in this version:
#   Vectorized (128-bit) global-memory loads / shared-memory stores for the
#   A and B tile staging.  Within each warp (fixed ty, tx = 0..31) the 32
#   elements needed from A (contiguous along k) and from B (contiguous
#   along n) are loaded using ld.global.v4.f32 / st.shared.v4.f32 by only
#   1-in-4 lanes (tx % 4 == 0), each fetching/storing 4 contiguous floats
#   at once.  This reduces the number of global memory transactions and
#   shared-memory store instructions issued per K-tile by 4x while
#   preserving the exact same shared-memory tile layout (and therefore the
#   same downstream compute loop), which matters a great deal here because
#   K is extremely large (many thousands of K-tile iterations per CTA).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

